WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.934
-Our next guest is
a Grammy nominated comedian

00:00:02.934 --> 00:00:04.667
who's latest special
"Hello, World"

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.501
is streaming now on Prime Video.

00:00:06.501 --> 00:00:07.734
Let's take a look.

00:00:07.734 --> 00:00:09.300
-So, one part
of your brain is smart

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.634
and the other part is dumb.

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:12.033
You can trick your own brain.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:14.434
That's how dumb
the dumb part is.

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:15.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.334
It's in the same head.

00:00:17.334 --> 00:00:18.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:19.968
That's embarrassing.

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:22.400
It's like, you don't
hear this going on, dude?

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.200
If you're in a bad mood,
they tell you to fake smile

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.300
and your dumb part
will think it's a good day.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.467
[ Laughter ]

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:32.167
I mean, you don't hear
the planning going on?

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:34.467
Like, you're a part
of the planning.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.434
-Please welcome to the show
the very funny Nate Bargatze.

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:40.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:47.334
♪♪

00:00:47.334 --> 00:00:54.200
♪♪

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.200
Welcome, Nate!

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.234
-Hey. Thank you, man.

00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:57.300
-I've been a fan
for a long time.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.501
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:58.501 --> 00:00:59.734
-I'm excited to be here, dude.

00:00:59.734 --> 00:01:01.100
This is fun.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.200
-You're from Tennessee,
you live in Tennessee,

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.434
but you you spent a fair amount
of time here in New York City.

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:08.367
-Yeah, I lived here for
eight and a half years,

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:11.234
like, 2004 to '12.

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:13.300
-And what were your first
impressions of the city?

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:15.000
-It was great. It's a lot, Dude.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.734
I mean, it's such a different
thing coming from Tennessee,

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:19.067
like, haven't left.

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:22.000
I remember first seeing when
people parallel parked,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.501
like, they bump cars, you know?

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:28.834
And me, I saw it and I was like,
I'm watching a hit and run.

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:33.567
I watched -- I wrote their
license plate down.

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:38.534
I was gonna call the NYPD,
who's probably the most buys.

00:01:38.534 --> 00:01:42.267
And just be like, "You won't
believe what's going on."

00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:44.434
And, like, I mean, I thought
it was like a giant thing,

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:46.367
and then you just learn
that's what --

00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:48.434
that's what everybody does.
That's what bumpers are for.

00:01:48.434 --> 00:01:49.767
-Yeah, that's right.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.067
-That's -- We were like,
"Nope that's what they're for."

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:53.000
-I actually became
unbelievable at it,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.267
and I remember I one time parked
and mine was touching two cars.

00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:58.567
So I got real good.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.701
I though, it fits --
If we can do it, I will do it.

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:01.834
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.834 --> 00:02:03.334
-If there's a skill
out there to be learned,

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.434
you might as well learn it.

00:02:04.434 --> 00:02:06.534
Now, I'm surprised
that you had this skill.

00:02:06.534 --> 00:02:08.501
A lot of people
I think know this now,

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:10.634
but when you're an early
comedian in the city,

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:12.200
one of the things you do
to get stage time

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.200
is you go out and you
sort of are a comedy barker,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.934
you try to get people
to go to shows.

00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:18.467
And sometimes if you're walking
around, certainly, like,

00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:20.868
Times Square, you see really
enthusiastic young comedians

00:02:20.868 --> 00:02:21.868
trying to convince people.

00:02:21.868 --> 00:02:22.901
You did that.

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:24.968
You don't quite have
that barker vibe.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:26.767
-Yeah, it's this energy...
-Yeah.

00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:28.701
-...that's just like,
"Hey, you wanna come --"

00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:29.868
I wouldn't even do that.

00:02:29.868 --> 00:02:31.167
"You wanna, like,
come to this comedy show?

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.200
And people were like, "No."

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.868
Like, they would
grab the flyer --

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:37.334
It was at Boston Comedy Club,
it was called New York,

00:02:37.334 --> 00:02:40.667
and they would get the flyer
and just drop it

00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:42.067
in front of me.

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:44.234
Like ju-- It was almost like
they just wanted to litter.

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:46.400
Like, they could have
just said no.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.234
But they were like, they just
wanted me to pick it up.

00:02:49.234 --> 00:02:50.634
-They wanted to
save other people

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:52.267
from having to be
asked this question.

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:53.701
-They grabbed them all
and they threw them.

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:56.567
They go, "Just go.
Move back home."

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:58.834
-This last special is wonderful.

00:02:58.834 --> 00:02:59.901
We were talking backstage.

00:02:59.901 --> 00:03:01.200
I think we have
very similar wives.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.901
Your wife seems to know
how everything works,

00:03:03.901 --> 00:03:06.501
very helpful around the house.

00:03:06.501 --> 00:03:08.133
I don't know anything.

00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:09.968
You make a great show
of the fact that

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:11.367
you don't know anything.

00:03:11.367 --> 00:03:14.133
And a boiler guy showed up
at you house.

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:15.968
and I've had this happen
before where they assume

00:03:15.968 --> 00:03:17.701
that you're the guy
with the information.

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:21.367
-Yeah, we say "water heater."
-Okay.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.734
-So, boiler, I didn't even know
what you're talking about.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:28.767
So, I mean,
I don't know what's happening.

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:31.968
But we, yeah, in the special,

00:03:31.968 --> 00:03:34.100
I talk about how our
water heater broke

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.367
and the guy who came over
was asking me where it was.

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:38.667
And I don't know where --
Like, I was like, I don't know.

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:42.100
And -- 'Cause it's like, I would
just walk by at the wrong time.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.467
A lot of times when --
'Cause she has people over
there,

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:47.267
just coming over
and doing stuff.

00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:48.934
She's always fixing something.

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:51.100
And they talk to me.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.767
And, like, I keep,
like, looking.

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:54.567
I'm like -- And she answers.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:55.767
But they keep looking at me.

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:57.133
I'm like, what are you
not getting, dude?

00:03:57.133 --> 00:04:00.801
Like, I don't know
these answers.

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:02.300
We have a new roof.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.200
And I found out
from my neighbor.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.767
A whole new roof.

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:11.000
I was, like, out in the
driveway, he goes,

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:12.000
"How's the new roof?"

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.300
And I go, "What's that?"

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.167
And then she went and got
a new roof when I was gone,

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:19.567
and I didn't even --

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:20.801
There was no, like, heads up.

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:22.567
Like, I'm not in --
Like, it didn't matter.

00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:24.434
It's not like I'm gonna
live differently.

00:04:24.434 --> 00:04:26.100
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.167
But I was --
It's embarrassing, you know,

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:31.000
to be sitting there
and be in my 40's.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.467
-I also, I mean, it seems like
you're the same way as me.

00:04:33.467 --> 00:04:35.901
Like, I would assume if my wife
decides we need a new roof,

00:04:35.901 --> 00:04:38.133
I agree, right?

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:39.467
-Yeah, oh yeah.
-I would never put my foot down

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:42.234
and be like,
"There -- Not on my --"

00:04:42.234 --> 00:04:44.033
"This roof is the roof
we're gonna have."

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:45.667
-No, I would -- Yeah.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:47.734
I stay out, and I would like to.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:49.467
I would like to do that,
to go, like,

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:53.033
"Over my dead body,
are we gonna get a new roof."

00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:54.601
I love this roof so much.

00:04:54.601 --> 00:04:55.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:57.934
I don't even know how to tell
if a roof's bad.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:04:59.133
Like, I don't --

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:00.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:02.534
Like, I mean, if it's just gone,

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.334
you know?

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:07.067
-If water poured in,
I would say we need a new roof.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:08.334
But other than that...

00:05:08.334 --> 00:05:10.400
-If she told me we don't,
I would say okay.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:12.100
Like, I would be like,
"Well, she knows."

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.133
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:05:13.133 --> 00:05:14.133
-She's like,
"Oh, this is a bucket.

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:15.434
You just get
a bucket for this."

00:05:15.434 --> 00:05:17.367
-You're like, "All right."
-"Where's the buckets?"

00:05:17.367 --> 00:05:19.767
That's where? I don't know.

00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:21.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.601 --> 00:05:24.534
-You -- But this is --
You used to read,

00:05:24.534 --> 00:05:26.167
so I'm surprised you don't know
about a water heater,

00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:28.434
'cause you used to do --
You were a meter reader. Yes?

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:29.501
-A water meter reader.

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:31.033
-Water meter reader.
-Yes.

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:34.267
And, so, when I first started,
like, this is the job I had.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:35.934
This is the job I'll go back to
when all this falls apart.

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:37.200
-Okay.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.267
This is a Tennessee job?

00:05:38.267 --> 00:05:40.100
-This is a Tennessee,
Wilson County,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.734
we read water meters, so,
like, you know, to see, like,

00:05:42.734 --> 00:05:45.334
how much water you used,
and you get billed for it.

00:05:45.334 --> 00:05:48.567
And, so, it was in 2001
and it was when 9/11 happened.

00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:51.267
So right after 9/11 happened,

00:05:51.267 --> 00:05:54.400
they got worried that they were
gonna come after our water

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.734
at Wilson County --
the terrorists.

00:05:56.734 --> 00:05:57.734
And...

00:05:57.734 --> 00:05:59.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:02.300
So, we --

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.467
This is all we --

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:05.400
For one year,
we would take shifts

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.601
and you would guard
the water towers.

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:12.100
And we spent the night just
begging the Taliban to try.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.467
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:15.901
Just --

00:06:15.901 --> 00:06:18.767
Come get in Mount Juliet's
water, dude.

00:06:18.767 --> 00:06:21.400
You gonna have the nerve

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.067
to poison Wilson County's water?

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:25.901
Not on our watch.

00:06:25.901 --> 00:06:28.534
We were out there all night
in the woods waiting for them.

00:06:28.534 --> 00:06:29.534
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:30.901
-Well, it was effective.

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:32.834
They didn't --
Word got back to them.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:33.834
They didn't come near it.

00:06:33.834 --> 00:06:35.000
-Ah, water's been great.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.300
-Yep.
-It's been great.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.501
-You, uh, you do tell a lot
of jokes about your wife.

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:43.133
I should note,
she comes out always...

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:44.901
I mean, you tell 'em.
You know.

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.200
She comes out
a lot smarter than you.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.701
-Oh, yeah, yeah.
-Makes it helpful,

00:06:47.701 --> 00:06:49.534
but do you run everything
by her ahead of time?

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:52.100
-Yeah, she knows everything.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.400
Like, I tell her --

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:54.701
Like, she knows all the jokes
that I'm gonna say.

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:56.167
Like, I don't want to surpri--

00:06:56.167 --> 00:06:57.300
Like, you know,
say anything mean, or, like,

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:58.801
you know, stuff like that.

00:06:58.801 --> 00:07:01.634
So she knows all the jokes, and
she's got a sense of humor of,

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:03.734
like, she likes making fun, too.

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:08.367
So it works that I can say the
stuff and I let her hear it.

00:07:08.367 --> 00:07:10.601
I may have one joke --
I don't have one,

00:07:10.601 --> 00:07:13.334
but it's like, if one was great,
though, it would be tough.

00:07:13.334 --> 00:07:16.767
Like, 'cause it's like, how hard
it is to get a good joke.

00:07:16.767 --> 00:07:18.334
If she's like,
"I don't like that one,"

00:07:18.334 --> 00:07:20.634
I was like, "Ah, it gets
a pretty big applause, so..."

00:07:20.634 --> 00:07:21.901
Like, I don't know.

00:07:21.901 --> 00:07:23.334
-"I mean, I did get you
a new roof."

00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:25.033
-I'd go -- Yeah!

00:07:25.033 --> 00:07:26.734
I'd go, "We'll get another one!
We'll get two roofs!"

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:28.100
-Yeah, you want one?
-Might as well put another one

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.601
on top of it. Double it up."

00:07:29.601 --> 00:07:30.634
-Double the roof.
-Why aren't more people

00:07:30.634 --> 00:07:31.734
not doing more roofs?

00:07:31.734 --> 00:07:33.067
You should do two roofs.

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:34.901
-You have a daughter.
-Yep.

00:07:34.901 --> 00:07:37.000
-Does -- Who gets
the laughs at home?

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.601
Does your --
Is your daughter funny?

00:07:38.601 --> 00:07:39.634
Does she think you're funny?

00:07:39.634 --> 00:07:40.901
-I don't think she --

00:07:40.901 --> 00:07:44.467
I mean, she gets that
people think I'm funny,

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:46.100
but she'd very funny.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:50.400
And she -- And I like it 'cause
she'll get my wife going,

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.634
and then once my wife starts
laughing, like, our daughter,

00:07:52.634 --> 00:07:54.501
she just doesn't let up.
It's like a show.

00:07:54.501 --> 00:07:56.968
So, it's, like, very fun to see,
like she just gets it,

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:59.601
and she's like, all right, I got
her, and it just keeps rolling.

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:02.467
She does, like, she does voices,

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:04.200
like very more
talented than I am.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.601
Like, I'm just this.

00:08:05.601 --> 00:08:07.634
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.634 --> 00:08:09.100
-Well --
-She can -- all over the place.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.367
-Well, next generations
sometimes are.

00:08:11.367 --> 00:08:12.567
It's a 2.0 situation.

00:08:12.567 --> 00:08:15.868
I remain a very big fan
of what you do on stage.

00:08:15.868 --> 00:08:17.000
Thank you so much
for being here, man.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.200
What a pleasure to meet you.
-Absolutely.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.133
-You guys, that's Nate Bargatze.

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:22.200
"Hello World" is streaming now
on Prime Video.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.968
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night"

